1
00:00:01,833 --> 00:00:04,271
In this video, we're going to talk about

2
00:00:04,271 --> 00:00:06,883
the human heart: how it is built, how

3
00:00:06,883 --> 00:00:09,270
blood moves through it, and what makes it

4
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:13,363
beat. We'll be precise, and connect each

5
00:00:13,363 --> 00:00:15,451
idea to what you see on the ward.

6
00:00:16,673 --> 00:00:19,223
By the end, you'll know the anatomy of its

7
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:21,636
chambers and valves, you'll be able to

8
00:00:21,636 --> 00:00:23,798
trace the route blood travels through both

9
00:00:23,798 --> 00:00:26,073
circulations, and you'll understand the

10
00:00:26,073 --> 00:00:28,611
electrical rhythm behind every beat.

11
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:35,429
Your heart beats about 100,000 times a

12
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:39,567
day. At rest it moves roughly 5 litres of

13
00:00:39,567 --> 00:00:41,005
blood every minute:

14
00:00:41,005 --> 00:00:43,280
more than 7,000 litres a day.

15
00:00:44,979 --> 00:00:46,829
And it never takes a day off.

16
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:50,355
To care for patients with heart disease,

17
00:00:50,392 --> 00:00:52,680
you first need to understand this pump.

18
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,237
So what exactly is the heart?

19
00:00:59,388 --> 00:01:01,551
It is a four-chambered muscular pump in

20
00:01:01,551 --> 00:01:03,913
the mediastinum, the space between the

21
00:01:03,913 --> 00:01:06,200
lungs, just behind the sternum.

22
00:01:07,444 --> 00:01:09,732
It is about the size of a closed fist and

23
00:01:09,732 --> 00:01:13,581
weighs roughly 250 to 350 grams.

24
00:01:14,756 --> 00:01:17,431
Its base faces up and to the right, and

25
00:01:17,431 --> 00:01:20,206
its apex points down and to the left.

26
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:25,808
Let's build the heart wall from the

27
00:01:25,808 --> 00:01:29,525
outside in! First, the fibrous

28
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:32,562
pericardium, a tough sac that anchors and

29
00:01:32,562 --> 00:01:33,862
protects the heart.

30
00:01:35,109 --> 00:01:37,434
Inside it, the parietal layer of the

31
00:01:37,434 --> 00:01:38,872
serous pericardium.

32
00:01:40,093 --> 00:01:42,606
Then the pericardial cavity, holding a

33
00:01:42,606 --> 00:01:44,781
film of fluid so the heart can beat

34
00:01:44,781 --> 00:01:46,030
without friction.

35
00:01:47,213 --> 00:01:50,288
Next, the epicardium, the visceral layer

36
00:01:50,288 --> 00:01:51,763
on the heart's surface.

37
00:01:52,965 --> 00:01:55,690
Then the thick myocardium, the muscle that

38
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:56,865
does the pumping.

39
00:01:58,069 --> 00:02:00,694
And finally the endocardium, the smooth

40
00:02:00,694 --> 00:02:02,831
lining of the chambers and valves.

41
00:02:06,767 --> 00:02:09,205
Here is the heart in section, facing the

42
00:02:09,205 --> 00:02:11,917
patient, so the patient's right side is on

43
00:02:11,917 --> 00:02:15,683
your left. Blood returning from the body

44
00:02:15,683 --> 00:02:17,433
enters the right atrium.

45
00:02:18,655 --> 00:02:21,155
It drops into the right ventricle below.

46
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:24,667
On the other side, the left atrium

47
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:26,867
receives blood from the lungs and passes

48
00:02:26,867 --> 00:02:29,392
it to the left ventricle, whose wall is by

49
00:02:29,392 --> 00:02:30,642
far the thickest.

50
00:02:31,863 --> 00:02:33,888
The ventricles are divided by the

51
00:02:33,888 --> 00:02:35,563
interventricular septum.

52
00:02:36,775 --> 00:02:39,225
And rising from the left ventricle is the

53
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:41,975
aorta, the body's largest artery.

54
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:48,487
Four valves keep blood moving one way.

55
00:02:49,732 --> 00:02:51,832
Between the right atrium and right

56
00:02:51,832 --> 00:02:54,232
ventricle sits the tricuspid valve.

57
00:02:55,532 --> 00:02:57,982
The pulmonary valve guards the exit to the

58
00:02:57,982 --> 00:03:01,832
lungs. On the left, the mitral valve

59
00:03:01,832 --> 00:03:04,332
separates the atrium from the ventricle.

60
00:03:05,548 --> 00:03:08,198
And the aortic valve opens into the aorta.

61
00:03:09,952 --> 00:03:12,377
Fine cords called chordae tendineae tie

62
00:03:12,377 --> 00:03:14,715
the cusps of the atrioventricular valves

63
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:17,202
to papillary muscles, so they cannot flip

64
00:03:17,202 --> 00:03:19,402
back when the ventricles contract.

65
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:24,808
Now follow the blood.

66
00:03:26,037 --> 00:03:28,524
Deoxygenated blood returns from the body

67
00:03:28,524 --> 00:03:30,487
through the venae cavae into the right

68
00:03:30,487 --> 00:03:34,061
atrium. The right ventricle pumps it

69
00:03:34,061 --> 00:03:36,311
through the pulmonary valve to the lungs,

70
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:38,098
where it picks up oxygen.

71
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:41,305
Oxygenated blood returns through the

72
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:44,067
pulmonary veins into the left atrium.

73
00:03:45,761 --> 00:03:47,861
The left ventricle then pumps it through

74
00:03:47,861 --> 00:03:50,998
the aortic valve into the aorta, and on to

75
00:03:50,998 --> 00:03:52,123
every tissue.

76
00:03:56,033 --> 00:03:58,270
Together, that makes two circuits in

77
00:03:58,270 --> 00:04:02,235
series. The pulmonary circulation carries

78
00:04:02,235 --> 00:04:04,122
blood from the right heart to the lungs

79
00:04:04,122 --> 00:04:05,747
and back to the left heart.

80
00:04:06,913 --> 00:04:09,175
The systemic circulation carries it from

81
00:04:09,175 --> 00:04:11,288
the left heart to the body tissues and

82
00:04:11,288 --> 00:04:12,763
back to the right heart.

83
00:04:14,437 --> 00:04:17,099
Every drop passes through both, one after

84
00:04:17,099 --> 00:04:17,837
the other.

85
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,950
A common mistake is to think arteries

86
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:26,663
always carry oxygenated blood and veins

87
00:04:26,663 --> 00:04:29,000
always carry deoxygenated blood.

88
00:04:30,216 --> 00:04:33,988
It isn't true. Arteries carry blood away

89
00:04:33,988 --> 00:04:36,338
from the heart; veins carry it toward the

90
00:04:36,338 --> 00:04:40,242
heart. So the pulmonary arteries carry

91
00:04:40,242 --> 00:04:42,767
deoxygenated blood to the lungs, and the

92
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:45,317
pulmonary veins bring oxygenated blood

93
00:04:45,317 --> 00:04:49,810
back. Name vessels by direction, not by

94
00:04:49,810 --> 00:04:50,635
oxygen.

95
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:56,275
What makes the heart beat?

96
00:04:57,424 --> 00:04:59,987
Each beat starts in the sinoatrial node,

97
00:05:00,049 --> 00:05:02,224
the natural pacemaker in the wall of the

98
00:05:02,224 --> 00:05:06,162
right atrium. The impulse spreads across

99
00:05:06,162 --> 00:05:08,612
both atria, and they contract.

100
00:05:09,864 --> 00:05:12,552
It then reaches the atrioventricular node,

101
00:05:12,664 --> 00:05:16,052
which holds it back for about 0.1 seconds,

102
00:05:16,102 --> 00:05:18,414
so the atria can finish emptying.

103
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:22,550
Then it races down the bundle of His,

104
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,800
splits into the right and left bundle

105
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,425
branches, and spreads through the Purkinje

106
00:05:27,425 --> 00:05:30,125
fibres, so the ventricles contract from

107
00:05:30,125 --> 00:05:31,537
the apex upward.

108
00:05:35,433 --> 00:05:37,533
Every part of this system can generate

109
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:40,258
beats on its own, at different rates.

110
00:05:41,521 --> 00:05:44,383
The SA node fires 60 to 100 times a

111
00:05:44,383 --> 00:05:48,914
minute. The AV node, 40 to 60.

112
00:05:50,145 --> 00:05:53,295
The Purkinje fibres, only 20 to 40.

113
00:05:55,029 --> 00:05:57,829
The fastest pacemaker sets the pace, which

114
00:05:57,829 --> 00:06:00,229
is why the SA node leads, and why a

115
00:06:00,229 --> 00:06:02,716
failing SA node leaves a slower backup

116
00:06:02,716 --> 00:06:03,379
rhythm.

117
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:09,979
On an ECG, this sequence becomes a

118
00:06:09,979 --> 00:06:14,213
familiar shape. The small P wave is atrial

119
00:06:14,213 --> 00:06:18,672
depolarisation. The tall QRS complex is

120
00:06:18,672 --> 00:06:21,672
ventricular depolarisation, normally under

121
00:06:21,672 --> 00:06:26,537
0.12 seconds. The T wave that follows is

122
00:06:26,537 --> 00:06:28,574
ventricular repolarisation.

123
00:06:30,227 --> 00:06:32,852
The PR interval, from the start of the P

124
00:06:32,852 --> 00:06:35,752
wave to the start of the QRS, is normally

125
00:06:35,752 --> 00:06:39,327
0.12 to 0.20 seconds.

126
00:06:43,233 --> 00:06:46,121
One heartbeat is a cycle of five phases.

127
00:06:47,353 --> 00:06:50,328
In atrial systole, the atria top up the

128
00:06:50,328 --> 00:06:54,703
ventricles. In isovolumetric contraction,

129
00:06:54,778 --> 00:06:56,965
the ventricles squeeze with every valve

130
00:06:56,965 --> 00:06:59,915
shut; the AV valves have just closed,

131
00:06:59,965 --> 00:07:02,290
giving the first heart sound, lub.

132
00:07:03,513 --> 00:07:06,476
During ventricular ejection, the semilunar

133
00:07:06,476 --> 00:07:08,563
valves open and blood leaves.

134
00:07:09,817 --> 00:07:12,692
In isovolumetric relaxation, they snap

135
00:07:12,692 --> 00:07:15,505
shut: the second heart sound, dub.

136
00:07:16,745 --> 00:07:19,470
And in ventricular filling, the AV valves

137
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:21,595
open and it all begins again.

138
00:07:25,533 --> 00:07:28,446
Both ventricles pump the same volume, but

139
00:07:28,446 --> 00:07:30,346
not against the same pressure.

140
00:07:31,525 --> 00:07:34,725
The left ventricle generates about 120

141
00:07:34,725 --> 00:07:37,125
millimetres of mercury, matched in the

142
00:07:37,125 --> 00:07:40,977
aorta. The right ventricle needs only

143
00:07:40,977 --> 00:07:44,477
about 25, matched in the pulmonary artery.

144
00:07:46,193 --> 00:07:48,618
That is why the left ventricular wall is

145
00:07:48,618 --> 00:07:50,418
about three times thicker.

146
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:56,112
Now a worked example:

147
00:07:56,162 --> 00:07:59,138
cardiac output, the volume each ventricle

148
00:07:59,138 --> 00:08:00,400
pumps per minute.

149
00:08:01,636 --> 00:08:04,436
It equals heart rate times stroke volume.

150
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,223
Take a resting heart rate of 70 beats per

151
00:08:08,223 --> 00:08:10,735
minute, and a stroke volume of 70

152
00:08:10,735 --> 00:08:12,260
millilitres per beat.

153
00:08:13,476 --> 00:08:17,664
Multiply: 70 times 70 gives 4,900

154
00:08:17,664 --> 00:08:19,226
millilitres per minute.

155
00:08:20,928 --> 00:08:24,241
That is about 4.9 litres, close to an

156
00:08:24,241 --> 00:08:27,441
adult's entire blood volume, every minute.

157
00:08:31,367 --> 00:08:33,829
The heart muscle has its own supply:

158
00:08:33,829 --> 00:08:36,254
the coronary arteries, which leave the

159
00:08:36,254 --> 00:08:39,092
aortic root just above the aortic valve.

160
00:08:40,359 --> 00:08:42,709
The left main coronary artery divides

161
00:08:42,709 --> 00:08:46,397
quickly. The left anterior descending

162
00:08:46,397 --> 00:08:48,447
artery feeds the front of the left

163
00:08:48,447 --> 00:08:50,147
ventricle and the septum.

164
00:08:51,359 --> 00:08:53,734
The circumflex wraps around to the side

165
00:08:53,734 --> 00:08:57,342
and back. The right coronary artery

166
00:08:57,342 --> 00:08:59,917
supplies the right heart, and usually the

167
00:08:59,917 --> 00:09:04,445
SA and AV nodes. The left ventricle is

168
00:09:04,445 --> 00:09:06,920
perfused mainly during diastole.

169
00:09:08,083 --> 00:09:10,370
And a blocked coronary artery starves the

170
00:09:10,370 --> 00:09:11,683
muscle beyond it:

171
00:09:11,695 --> 00:09:13,470
a myocardial infarction.

172
00:09:17,367 --> 00:09:18,867
Time for a knowledge check.

173
00:09:20,095 --> 00:09:22,407
Why is the wall of the left ventricle so

174
00:09:22,407 --> 00:09:24,170
much thicker than the wall of the right

175
00:09:24,170 --> 00:09:27,421
ventricle? Pause here and think it

176
00:09:27,421 --> 00:09:27,971
through.

177
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:39,700
Because the left ventricle must push blood

178
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:42,225
through the entire systemic circulation,

179
00:09:42,288 --> 00:09:45,012
against a pressure of about 120

180
00:09:45,012 --> 00:09:46,562
millimetres of mercury.

181
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:50,118
The right ventricle only has to reach the

182
00:09:50,118 --> 00:09:52,743
lungs, against about 25.

183
00:09:54,488 --> 00:09:56,926
The systemic circuit is long, with high

184
00:09:56,926 --> 00:10:00,424
resistance. The pulmonary circuit is

185
00:10:00,424 --> 00:10:02,262
short, with low pressure.

186
00:10:03,496 --> 00:10:05,858
And more pressure demands more muscle.

187
00:10:09,767 --> 00:10:13,797
Let's recap! Four chambers work as two

188
00:10:13,797 --> 00:10:17,825
pumps in series. Four valves keep blood

189
00:10:17,825 --> 00:10:20,450
moving one way, from the venae cavae to

190
00:10:20,450 --> 00:10:22,500
the lungs, and from the lungs to the

191
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:26,316
aorta. And every beat is timed by the

192
00:10:26,316 --> 00:10:28,953
conduction system, from the SA node to the

193
00:10:28,953 --> 00:10:31,603
Purkinje fibres, which you can read on the

194
00:10:31,603 --> 00:10:36,186
ECG. Know this pump well, and every

195
00:10:36,186 --> 00:10:38,111
cardiac assessment you make will make

196
00:10:38,111 --> 00:10:38,811
sense.

